\section{Heritability across generations}{

The first generation's phenotype defines the trait: \code{\link[=select_ind]{select_ind()}} stores its
causal loci, effects and residual variance on the selected individuals (see
\code{\link[=population_trait]{population_trait()}}), and their progeny inherit it. In later generations
the \code{phenotype} callback's \code{\link[=simulate_phenotype]{simulate_phenotype()}} reuses that trait
(\code{refit = FALSE}, its default for a population that carries one): the genetic
value stays on the base population's scale and the residual variance is held
fixed, so the heritability falls as selection and inbreeding exhaust genetic
variance, as in AlphaSimR's \code{setPheno(varE = )} and the Bancic et al. scripts.
The callback's \code{h2} and layer settings are then ignored (silently when they
match the trait, as a callback written for the founders does).

Write \code{simulate_phenotype(p, ..., refit = TRUE)} in the callback for the
pre-2.0.0.9003 rule: the genetic layers are re-standardised to their \code{prop}
and the residual re-fitted to \code{h2} in \strong{every} generation, so the genetic
share of the phenotype does not decay: for a purely additive model the
accuracy of mass selection, \code{cor(P, BV)}, stays near \eqn{\sqrt{h^2}} (with
dominance it declines as the additive part of the re-fitted genetic variance
shrinks). A phenotype with a \code{\link[=vqtl]{vqtl()}} or \code{\link[=transcriptome]{transcriptome()}} layer has no
storable trait and is always re-fitted.

\code{history$differential} and \code{history$intensity} are measured on the criterion
